# ec/tools/reassembly_checked_bound.py --check, wired into the cheap gate.
# Prepared here rather than landed, for the reason
# docs/ci/agent-gates-gap-text-check.patch gives: `.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh`
# is copied from the agent-pipeline template and the pipeline's push token has no
# `workflow` scope, so a branch editing it fails at the end of the PR rather than
# at the start of it.
#
# To land it:
#
#   git apply docs/ci/agent-gates-reassembly-bound-check.patch
#
# and that is the whole change.
#
# Why this one at all (issue #229). `verify_reassembly.check_one()` returned one
# count under two names: `instructions_checked` is how many instructions
# `to_sdas()` translated and handed to sdas8051, and how many of those bytes
# reached a comparison against the firmware image was not counted at all. That
# change made the tool carry both counts and split the two cases that both
# answered to `assembler-gap`; this puts a check on the committed report's own
# columns saying which rows compared nothing, so the corrected headline in
# `ec/ghidra/README.md` and `docs/findings.md` is re-derived on every commit
# rather than resting on a write-up. It reads `ec/ghidra/reassembly.csv` and
# `ec/decompiled/listing-index.csv` with the standard library's `csv` and the
# committed `.asm` listings, and nothing else: no assembler, no Ghidra, no
# network, no firmware.
#
# WHERE IT GOES, and why not into the tool loop it reads like it belongs in.
# Every position in that loop's tool list and every line of the `gate` list
# below is already another prepared patch's context:
# agent-gates-0751-self-test.patch and agent-gates-gap-text-check.patch both
# write into the tool list, agent-gates-disasm8051-self-test.patch rewrites its
# last entry, and capture-claims, pin-table-rows, testdata-row-claims and
# findings-frozen all write into the `gate` list or into check_doc_links(). A
# fifth hunk in either list cannot be applied alongside them in either order,
# which is what tools/test_agent_gates_patches.py fails on and what the
# findings-frozen patch's own header says about the gate list -- "composition,
# not tidiness". The tail of `check_ghidra_tooling()`, after the `done` and
# before the scratch directory is removed, is the one region no prepared patch's
# hunk reaches. It is the right gate regardless: every arm of that loop is a
# committed-text check under python3 with no assembler and no Ghidra, and this
# is one of those.
#
# COST. 0.15 s here over three runs on 2026-10-01, against a cheap tier §14e's
# table records at 5.9 s. One runner's figure; the ratio is the durable part,
# which is the caveat `docs/agent-pipeline.md` item 6 gives for `call_graph.py`
# too.
#
# ONE THING TO KNOW BEFORE LANDING IT, and the comment at the call site says the
# same thing where the next reader of the gate will see it. `--check` FAILS on
# what a commit can fix -- a cell that does not hold an integer, a `detail`
# shape it does not recognise, an anchor it could not read -- and PRINTS the
# overclaim rather than failing on it. That is the one deliberate departure from
# what the name suggests. The strict assertion would be red on the tree this is
# cut from, and would stay red until #157 re-reports `reassembly.csv` under the
# pinned nix build: a row whose assembled listing had no entry reports checked
# instructions and compared none of them under *any* build, because the
# committed CSV has no column that can tell the two apart yet.
# docs/ci/agent-gates-gap-text-check.patch records what happens to a gate that
# is red on the day it is landed -- "the cheapest way to make a gate get
# switched off" -- and that is the whole of the reasoning. `--fail-on-overclaim`
# is the strict form and is wired nowhere;
# `ec/tools/test_reassembly_checked_bound.py` holds it red on the committed
# tree, so it is not a flag nobody can tell works.
#
# What it buys, and what it does not. It keeps the arithmetic of the corrected
# claim honest, and it is what fails if a row's columns stop supporting the
# reading -- which is what makes the correction falsifiable rather than a
# sentence nobody re-derives. It does not make the 1:1 claim stronger, it does
# not settle why those rows' listings carried no entry (three candidates, all
# live, in `docs/findings/reassembly-checked-counts-comparisons.md`), and it
# reads no capture, opens no EC and reads back no register.

diff --git a/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh b/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
index ce9f096..109aefc 100755
--- a/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
+++ b/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
@@ -284,6 +284,37 @@ check_ghidra_tooling() {
         ;;
     esac
   done
+  # What the reassembly report says about its own columns: which rows whose
+  # detail reads `no bytes emitted at <addr>` compared nothing, and the ceiling
+  # that leaves for the instructions that did. issue #229 made check_one()
+  # count bytes compared rather than instructions handed over, and without this
+  # the corrected headline in ec/ghidra/README.md and docs/findings.md would be
+  # re-derived by nobody. Cheap tier for the reason every arm above gives: the
+  # committed report, the committed .asm listings and the standard library's
+  # csv. No assembler, no Ghidra, no network.
+  #
+  # `--check` fails on what a commit can fix -- a cell that does not hold an
+  # integer, a detail shape it does not recognise, an anchor it could not read --
+  # and *prints* the overclaim with its rows named rather than failing on it.
+  # That is deliberate and is the one thing to know before landing it: the
+  # strict form would be red on the tree it is landed onto, for a reason no
+  # commit to this repository can fix until #157 re-reports reassembly.csv under
+  # the pinned build, and a gate that is red on day one is the cheapest way to
+  # get a gate switched off. docs/ci/agent-gates-gap-text-check.patch records
+  # that happening once. `--fail-on-overclaim` is the strict form, wired
+  # nowhere.
+  #
+  # Called here rather than from the tool loop above, and the reason is
+  # composition: every position in that loop's list and every line of the
+  # `gate` list below is already another prepared patch's context
+  # (tools/test_agent_gates_patches.py holds them, and
+  # docs/findings.md's check_findings_frozen patch says the same about the gate
+  # list). This is the gate the tool belongs to either way -- it reads the same
+  # committed text every arm above reads -- and this is the one place in the
+  # file no prepared patch's hunk reaches.
+  if [ -f ec/tools/reassembly_checked_bound.py ]; then
+    python3 ec/tools/reassembly_checked_bound.py --check || rc=1
+  fi
   rm -rf "$scratch"
   return "$rc"
 }
